  "question": {
    "text": "Let $g(x)=\\int_{x}^{2 x} \\frac{f(t)}{t} d t$ where $x>0$ and $f$ be continuous function and $f(2 x)=f(x)$, then"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "$g(x)$ is strictly increasing function",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\mathrm{g}(\\mathrm{x})$ is strictly decreasing function",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\mathrm{g}(\\mathrm{x})$ is constant function",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\mathrm{g}(\\mathrm{x})$ is not derivable function",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "$g(x)=\\int_{x}^{2 x} \\frac{f(t)}{t} d t$\r\n$g^{\\prime}(x)=\\frac{f(2 x)}{2 x} \\cdot 2^{\\prime}-\\frac{f(x)}{x} \\cdot 1=\\frac{f(2 x)-f(x)}{x}=\\frac{f(x)-f(x)}{x}[\\because f(2 x)=f(x)]$\r\n$g^{\\prime}(x)=0$\r\n$g(x)=$ constant."
  }
}